<header><p class="enc-kicker">Religion / idea · deity</p><h1 class="enc-title">Huitzilopochtli</h1></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Huitzilopochtli was the Aztec god of the sun and war and the patron deity of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mexica">Mexica</a>. According to tradition, he guided the Mexica on their migration to the site of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/tenochtitlan">Tenochtitlan</a>. His cult, centred on the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/templo-mayor">Templo Mayor</a>, was closely bound up with warfare and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/human-sacrifice">human sacrifice</a> in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/aztec-empire">Aztec Empire</a>.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/huitzilopochtli.jpg" alt="Huitzilopochtli" width="320" height="372" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Huitzilopochtli_telleriano.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Origins</h2><p>The name combines huitzilin, hummingbird, and opochtli, left-hand side, and is often translated Left-Handed Hummingbird or Hummingbird of the South. Scholars disagree about its full significance. In one account he was the smallest of four sons of the creator couple, with <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/quetzalcoatl">Quetzalcoatl</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/xipe-totec">Xipe Totec</a> and Tezcatlipoca as brothers. In another, the goddess Coatlicue was made pregnant by a ball of feathers on Mount Coatepec. Her other children conspired to kill her, and Huitzilopochtli burst from her womb in armour, beheaded his sister Coyolxauhqui and scattered his brothers across the sky.</p><p>In the legend of the Aubin Codex, Huitzilopochtli told the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/aztec-empire">Aztecs</a> to leave <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/aztlan">Aztlan</a> and to call themselves <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mexica">Mexica</a>, and guided them on their journey. He told them to build their city where an eagle perched on a cactus, eating a serpent. They settled on an island in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/lake-texcoco">Lake Texcoco</a>, the site of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/tenochtitlan">Tenochtitlan</a>.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">What it is</h2><p>Huitzilopochtli was the solar and war deity of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mexica">Mexica</a> and patron god of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/tenochtitlan">Tenochtitlan</a>. In myth he is the sun, his siblings the stars and the moon, and the daily sunrise was read as his battle with them. Originally of little importance to the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/nahuas">Nahuas</a>, he was raised by <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/tlacaelel">Tlacaelel</a>'s reform of religion to the level of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/quetzalcoatl">Quetzalcoatl</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/tlaloc">Tlaloc</a> and Tezcatlipoca. He was shown as a hummingbird or as a figure with a hummingbird helmet and a blue-painted body. Warriors who died in battle or as sacrifices were thought to join him as hummingbirds.</p><p>The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/templo-mayor">Templo Mayor</a> of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/tenochtitlan">Tenochtitlan</a> was dedicated to him and to the rain god <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/tlaloc">Tlaloc</a>, his shrine red and on the south side, Tlaloc's blue and on the north. At the base of the stairway lay the Coyolxauhqui stone, showing the dismembered goddess, so that the temple recalled the mountain of Coatepec.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">How it was used</h2><p>Aztec worship held that the sun needed nourishment in human blood and hearts, and that the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mexica">Mexica</a> as people of the sun had to provide it. Captives were sacrificed on a stone, their hearts cut out and their bodies sent down the pyramid, and a warrior who took a victim rose in the social hierarchy. The month of Panquetzaliztli was his festival, with processions, dances and sacrifices of victims dressed in his costume, and a statue of amaranth seeds and honey was eaten in pieces. The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/aztec-empire">Aztecs</a> reported that about 20,400 prisoners were sacrificed at the reconsecration of the Great Pyramid in 1487, a figure some scholars accept and others consider Aztec propaganda.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Huītzilōpōchtli” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>The Mexica were recast as the chosen people of the sun and war god <b>Huitzilopochtli</b>, whose continued existence required the nourishment of human sacrifice, and perpetual war became a cosmic necessity.</blockquote><a href="/empire/aztec-empire">Aztec Empire · Foundation</a></li><li><blockquote>The Aztecs also worshipped Tezcatlipoca, “Smoking Mirror”, the powerful god of night, fate and sorcery, and <b>Huitzilopochtli</b>, the sun and war god who was the patron of the Mexica.</blockquote><a href="/religion/mesoamerican-religion">Mesoamerican religion · Beliefs &amp; Practice</a></li><li><blockquote>He led the cult of <b>Huitzilopochtli</b>, and rulers were held to govern by divine right.</blockquote><a href="/societies/aztec-empire">Social Hierarchy of the Aztec Empire</a></li></ul></section>
